re is provided by exact and numerical\nL54@P2: calculations of the Mahler measure for a family of Laurent polynomials generated by rank 2 cluster algebras,\nL55@P2: for a recurrence of third order related to the Markoff numbers, and for the Somos-4 recurrence. Also, for the\nL56@P2: sequence of Laurent polynomials associated with the Kronecker quiver (the cluster algebra of affine type A˜ 1)\nL57@P2: we prove a precise formula for the leading order asymptotics of the logarithmic Mahler measure, which grows\nL58@P2: linearly.\nL59@P2: KEYWORDS\nL60@P2: Mahler measure; Laurent\nL61@P2: property; algebraic entropy;\nL62@P2: cluster algebra\nL63@P2: 1. Introduction, definitions, and main conjecture\nL64@P2: Given P(x1, ... , xN) ∈ C[x±1\nL65@P2: 1 , ... , x±1\nL66@P2: N ]\\{0}, a nonzero Laurent polynomial in N variables with complex coefficients, its logarithmic\nL67@P2: Mahler measure is defined to be\nL68@P2: m(P) :=\nL69@P2: \u0002\nL70@P2: TN\nL71@P2: log |P(x1, ... , xn)| d\u0002, (1.1)\nL72@P2: obtained by integrating over all points on the N-torus with respect to the Haar measure\nL73@P2: d\u0002 = dt1 ∧···∧ dtN\nL74@P2: (2πi)N (1.2)\nL75@P2: for (x1, ... , xN) = (eit1 , ... , eitN ) ∈ TN with local coordinates tj ∈ (−π, π], which can be considered as the restriction to the real\nL76@P2: torus of the log-canonical meromorphic N-form\nL77@P2: d\u0002 = dx1 ∧···∧ dxN\nL78@P2: (2πi)N \u0003N\nL79@P2: j=1 xj\nL80@P2: (1.3)\nL81@P2: on (C∗)N (or CN), with i = √−1. The name Mahler measure is also given to the quantity M(P) = exp m(P), but henceforth we will\nL82@P2: use the name without qualification to refer to the logarithmic version. For a polynomial P(x) ∈ C[x] in one variable of degree d, with\nL83@P2: leading coefficient c \u0006= 0 and roots αj, j = 1, ... , d, the Mahler measure is found explicitly by Jensen’s formula to be\nL84@P2: m(P) = log |c| +\u0004\nL85@P2: d\nL86@P2: j=1\nL87@P2: [log |αj|]+ (1.4)\nL88@P2: (here [a]+ = max(a, 0)), and while the existence of the N-variable integral in (1.1) is not immediately obvious, it is guaranteed by a\nL89@P2: result of Lawton [43], which shows that this reduces to the case of one variable, in the sense that\nL90@P2: m\nL91@P2: \u0005\nL92@P2: P(x1, ... , xN)\nL93@P2: \u0006\nL94@P2: = lim\nL95@P2: k2,...,kN→∞ m\nL96@P2: \u0005\nL97@P2: P(x, xk2 , ... , xkN )\nL98@P2: \u0006\nL99@P2: . (1.5)\nL100@P2: CONTACT Andrew N. W. Hone anwh@kent.ac.uk School of Mathematics, Statistics & Actuarial Science, University of Kent, Canterbury CT2 7FS, UK.\nL101@P2: © 2025 The Author(s). Published with license by Taylor and Francis Group, LLC\nL102@P2: This is an Open Access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/), which permits unrestricted use, distribution,\nL103@P2: and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited. The terms on which this article has been published allow the posting of the Accepted Manuscript in a repository by the\nL104@P2-3: author(s) or with their consent.2 A. N. W. HONE\nL105@P3: In particular, this implies that m(P) ≥ 0 for Laurent polynomials with integer coefficients. There are many interesting arithmetical\nL106@P3: questions concerning Mahler measure, like Lehmer’s problem about the smallest nonzero value of m(P) for P(x) ∈ Z[x], as well as\nL107@P3: the fact that explicit evaluations of m(P) for specific multivariable polynomials P can be expressed in terms of L-functions or zeta\nL108@P3: functions, or volumes of hyperbolic polyhedra [5, 11, 41]. The first such example in two variables is due to Smyth [57], who obtained\nL109@P3: the formula\nL110@P3: m(x1 + x2 + 1) = L\nL111@P3: \nL112@P3: (χ−3, −1) (1.6)\nL113@P3: in terms of the L-function for the Dirichlet character χ−3.\nL114@P3: Cluster algebras, introduced in [14], are a class of commutative algebras which, rather than being specified a priori, have\nL115@P3: distinguished sets of generators called clusters, which are produced recursively from an initial N-tuple of generators x = (x1, ... , xN)\nL116@P3: by a process called mutation. One of the basic features of cluster algebras is the Laurent property: the N elements in each cluster are\nL117@P3: Laurent polynomials in the initial generators, with integer coefficients. In fact, Zelevinsky once gave the following informal definition\nL118@P3: [62]: A cluster algebra is a machine for generating nontrivial Laurent polynomials. More precisely, a coefficient-free cluster algebra\nL119@P3: A = A(x, B) of rank N is generated by starting from a seed (x, B) consisting of an initial cluster x of N variables and a matrix\nL120@P3: B ∈ MatN(Z) that is skew-symmetrizable, in the sense that there exists a diagonal matrix of positive integers D such that DB is skew\u0002symmetric. Then for each integer k ∈ [1, N] there is a mutation μk, an involution which produces a new seed (x\nL121@P3: \nL122@P3: , B\nL123@P3: ) = μk(x, B),\nL124@P3: where B\nL125@P3:  = (b\nL126@P3: \nL127@P3: ij) with\nL128@P3: b\nL129@P3: \nL130@P3: ij =\nL131@P3: \u0007\nL132@P3: −bij if i = k or j = k,\nL133@P3: bij + sgn(bik)[bikbkj]+ otherwise, (1.7)\nL134@P3: and x\nL135@P3:  = (x\nL136@P3: \nL137@P3: j\nL138@P3: ) with\nL139@P3: x\nL140@P3: \nL141@P3: j =\nL142@P3: \u0007\nL143@P3: x−1\nL144@P3: k\nL145@P3: \u0005 \u0003N\nL146@P3: j=1 x\nL147@P3: [bkj]+\nL148@P3: j + \u0003N\nL149@P3: j=1 x\nL150@P3: [−bkj]+\nL151@P3: j\nL152@P3: \u0006 for j = k\nL153@P3: xj for j \u0006= k. (1.8)\nL154@P3: Now for an arbitrary composition of mutations μ = μkn ◦···◦ μk1 of any length |μ| = n ≥ 0, the Laurent property says that every\nL155@P3: element of the cluster μ(x) belongs to Z[x±1\nL156@P3: 1 , ... , x±1\nL157@P3: N ], and the cluster algebra A is defined to be the Z-algebra generated by all cluster\nL158@P3: variables in all clusters, that is A = Z\nL159@P3: \b\nL160@P3: ∪\nL161@P3: μ\nL162@P3: μ(x)\nL163@P3: . The cluster algebra A is determined by the seed (x, B) up to mutation equivalence,\nL164@P3: i.e. A(x, B) is isomorphic to A(x\nL165@P3: \nL166@P3: , B\nL167@P3: ) for (x, B) ∼ (x\nL168@P3: , B\nL169@P3: ), where the equivalence relation means that (x\nL170@P3: , B\nL171@P3: ) = μ(x, B) for some\nL172@P3: composition of mutations μ.\nL173@P3: For discrete dynamical systems defined by iteration of a rational map ϕ in dimension N, there are various notions of entropy\nL174@P3: that are used to measure the growth of complexity of the iterates. Letting deg ϕn denote the maximum of the degrees of the rational\nL175@P3: functions defining the components of ϕn(x), the algebraic entropy was defined in [4] as follows.\nL176@P3: Definition 1.1. The algebraic entropy of a rational map ϕ is given by\nL177@P3: E := limn→∞\nL178@P3: log deg ϕn\nL179@P3: n\nL180@P3: . (1.9)\nL181@P3: Note that the existence of the above limit is guaranteed by the subadditive property of the log degrees and Fekete’s lemma [12]. This\nL182@P3: measure of growth was first used extensively to study certain birational transformations arising in statistical mechanics (see [1, 3] and\nL183@P3: references therein). For birational maps P2 → P2 it is possible to perform blowups at singular points and reduce the computation\nL184@P3: of E to a calculation in terms of the induced linear action of the pullback ϕ∗ on the cohomology of the resulting enlarged space\nL185@P3: [10, 58], but in dimension N > 2 the problem is more difficult, because where the map has singularities it is not always possible to\nL186@P3: remove exceptional hypersurfaces from the dynamical system by blowups. In general, numerical computation of the algebraic entropy\nL187@P3: of ϕ is extremely computationally intensive, because it requires exact calculation of the iterates of ϕ, and generically deg ϕn grows\nL188@P3: exponentially with n.\nL189@P3: Due to the fact that matrix mutations (1.7) change the exponents that appear in (1.8), and because there are N possible mutations\nL190@P3: that can be applied to any cluster, generically a seed in a cluster algebra A defines a set of evolutions on an N-regular tree rather than\nL191@P3: a single dynamical system (apart from the so-called bipartite belt when N = 2) [14]; nevertheless, one can define a corresponding\nL192@P3: notion of algebraic entropy, analogous to (1.9), via\n"
